<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 4.7 Extreme Values and Saddle Points.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  extreme-values-saddle-points.ptx of the full lecture notes; every
  xml:id here is prefixed with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: every video is kept, since the surfaces cannot be drawn by
  hand and a reader checks their classification against them.  The
  prefigure diagrams that set a problem up (the open disk, the traces of
  the saddle, the rectangle with its four edges, the stability
  cross-sections) are reproduced in full.  The plot of the critical
  points and the plot of the candidate points ARE the answers to their
  examples, so those two become empty grids to mark up.

  No hints (Nov 2026, at the instructor's request), as in 4.6: no
  instruction line says how to do a derivation or an example, no box row
  names a method, and no caption, figure label or alt text gives an answer
  away; a solution keeps at most a pointer to the figure that checks it.
  Each task gets ONE blank workspace with a last row for the result.  So
  the captions of the videos describe the surface only, and the diagrams
  leave out the labels of the full notes that state a result (the
  inequality in the disk, "Local maximum", the equations of the saddle's
  traces, the interior critical point of the rectangle, "stable" and
  "unstable"); the stability diagram follows the box it checks.

  Nov 2026: the five diagrams kept in full are Asymptote now, the same
  figures as in the full notes.  The definition of a saddle point opens the
  subsection "Saddle Point" (formerly "A Saddle Point Example"), and two
  examples follow the definition of a critical point, each with one blank
  workspace; their graphs show the surface only, without the critical
  point, the tangent plane or the trace that the full notes mark.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-fmv-extreme-values" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Extreme Values and Saddle Points</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Understand what it means for a function of two variables to have a local
maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point, and visualize each on the
surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Recognize critical points as the candidates for local extrema, and understand
why a vanishing gradient is necessary but not sufficient for an extremum.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand the idea behind the Second Derivative Test and what it reveals
about the local shape of a surface near a critical point.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Distinguish local extrema from absolute extrema, and understand why the
absolute extrema of a function on a closed, bounded region may occur on the
boundary.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret extreme values in a physical setting, such as the equilibrium and
stability of a system.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-local-extrema">
<title>Local Maximum and Local Minimum</title>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-local-extrema">
<title>Local Maximum and Local Minimum</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let the domain of the function <m>f(x,y)</m> contain the point
<m>(a,b)</m>. Then
</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>f(a,b)</m> is a <term>local minimum</term> if
<m>f(a,b) \le f(x,y)</m> for all the points <m>(x,y)</m> in an open
disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f(a,b)</m> is a <term>local maximum</term> if
<m>f(a,b) \ge f(x,y)</m> for all the points <m>(x,y)</m> in an open
disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
Test the definition on two surfaces you already know,
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m> and <m>f(x,y) = -x^2 - y^2</m>, at the origin.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,8.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {$x^2+y^2$ at the origin: \qquad\qquad $-x^2-y^2$ at the origin:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for what each of the two functions has at the origin.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-local-min-video">
<caption>
The paraboloid <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m> near the origin.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-local-min"
       source="videos/local_min_paraboloid_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/local_min_paraboloid_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-local-min-disk">
<caption>
An open disk in the domain of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m> centered at
<m>(0,0)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="50%">
<shortdescription>An open disk centred at the origin.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
An open disk in the x y plane, centered at the origin, drawn with a dashed red boundary and shaded gray. The center point 0, 0 is marked in green.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-skel-ev-local-min-disk"><![CDATA[
// An open disk centred at (0,0) in the domain of f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2. The full
// notes also print the inequality that holds in it, which is left out here.
import graph;
unitsize(2.4cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

// the open disk: shaded, with a dashed boundary because the boundary is left out
path disk = circle((0, 0), 1.1);
fill(disk, gray(0.85));
draw(disk, red + linewidth(1.4pt) + linetype("6 4"));

axes(-1.55, 1.55, -1.55, 1.55, "$x$", "$y$");

dot((0, 0), rgb(0, 0.5, 0) + linewidth(5pt));
label("$(0,0)$", (0, 0), NE, rgb(0, 0.5, 0));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-local-max-video">
<caption>
The paraboloid <m>z = -x^2 - y^2</m> near the origin.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-local-max"
       source="videos/local_max_paraboloid_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/local_max_paraboloid_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-local-max-trace">
<caption>
The trace of the surface <m>z = -x^2 - y^2</m> in the plane
<m>y = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>A parabola through the origin opening downward.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The parabola z equals negative x squared, opening downward, which is the trace of the surface z equals negative x squared minus y squared in the plane y equals 0. The origin is marked in green.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-skel-ev-local-max-trace"><![CDATA[
// The trace of z = -x^2 - y^2 in the plane y = 0, the parabola z = -x^2. The full
// notes also label the origin as the local maximum, which is left out here.
import graph;
unitsize(2.6cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

axes(-1.5, 1.5, -1.85, 0.6, "$x$", "$z$");

real g(real x) { return -x^2; }
draw(graph(g, -1.3, 1.3), blue + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("$z=-x^2$", (0.95, g(0.95)), E, blue);

dot((0, 0), rgb(0, 0.5, 0) + linewidth(5pt));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-first-derivative-test">
<title>Theorem I</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> has a local maximum or minimum at an interior point
<m>(a,b)</m> of its domain and if the first partial derivatives exist
there, then
<md xml:id="eq-skel-first-derivative-test" number="yes">
f_x(a,b) = f_y(a,b) = 0.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Check <xref ref="thm-skel-first-derivative-test"/> on
<m>f(x,y) = x^2+y^2</m>, and say which way the implication of the
theorem runs.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {the implication runs:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the direction of the implication in the theorem.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-critical-point">
<title>Critical Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
An interior point of the domain of a function <m>f(x,y)</m> where both
<m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> are zero or where one or both of <m>f_x</m>
and <m>f_y</m> do not exist is a <term>critical point</term> of
<m>f</m>. Note that not every critical point is a local extremum.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-critical-point-zero-partials">
<title>A Critical Point Where Both Partial Derivatives Are Zero</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the critical points of the function
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 2y + 3</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compare with <xref ref="fig-skel-critical-point-zero-partials"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {the critical points:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the critical points of f.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-critical-point-zero-partials">
<caption>
The graph of <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 2y + 3</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>A bowl-shaped surface opening upward above the x y plane.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A gray paraboloid opening upward, the graph of f, drawn over a disc of the
x y plane in the first quadrant, with the x, y and z axes.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-skel-ev-critical-point-zero-partials"><![CDATA[
// The paraboloid z = x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 2y + 3, drawn as in the full notes but
// without the critical point, its tangent plane or the guide lines, which
// are the answer to the example.
import graph3;
// Keep HTML interactive; use vector rendering only for print output.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(11cm,9cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=(7,-5,4),up=Z);
currentlight=light(gray(0.6),specularfactor=0.2,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));
pen ax=gray(0.25)+linewidth(0.6pt);

// the surface over the disc of radius 1.5 centred at (1,1)
triple bowl(pair q) { return (1 + q.x*cos(q.y), 1 + q.x*sin(q.y), 1 + q.x^2); }
draw(surface(bowl, (0, 0), (1.5, 2pi), 8, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=gray(0.75)+opacity(0.35),
     meshpen=gray(0.55)+opacity(0.4)+linewidth(0.3pt));
label("$z=f(x,y)$", bowl((1.5, 0.3)), 2*E, gray(0.4));

draw(Label("$x$",1),O--(3.2,0,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),O--(0,3.2,0),ax,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),O--(0,0,3.7),ax,Arrow3);
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

