<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-absolute-extrema">
<title>Absolute Maximum and Absolute Minimum</title>

<p>
In the next example, we will see how to find absolute extrema of a
function <m>f(x,y)</m> over a closed bounded region <m>R</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-absolute-extrema">
<title>Absolute Extrema over a Rectangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 - 2xy + 2y</m> on the rectangle
<md xml:id="eq-skel-rectangle-region" number="yes">
R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 3,\; 0 \le y \le 2\}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compare with <xref ref="fig-skel-absolute-extrema-surface-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,19.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {the absolute maximum: \qquad\qquad the absolute minimum:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the absolute maximum and minimum values.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-rectangle-region-video">
<caption>
The closed bounded rectangle
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 3,\; 0 \le y \le 2\}</m> with its four
edges <m>L_1</m>, <m>L_2</m>, <m>L_3</m>, and <m>L_4</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-rectangle-region"
       source="videos/rectangle_region_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/rectangle_region_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-rectangle-region">
<caption>
The rectangle <m>R</m> and its four edges.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>A rectangle with its four edges labelled.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The rectangle R in the x y plane, with corners at the origin, 3, 0, 3, 2, and 0, 2, shaded gray. Its four edges are labeled L 1 along the bottom, L 2 along the right, L 3 along the top, and L 4 along the left.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-skel-ev-rectangle-region"><![CDATA[
// The rectangle R = [0,3] x [0,2] with its four edges L1, L2, L3, L4. The full notes
// also mark the interior critical point, which is left out here.
import graph;
unitsize(1.9cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

fill(box((0, 0), (3, 2)), gray(0.87));
axes(-0.6, 3.9, -0.6, 2.8, "$x$", "$y$");

pen c1 = blue, c2 = red, c3 = rgb(0, 0.5, 0), c4 = rgb(0.9, 0.5, 0);
draw((0, 0)--(3, 0), c1 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((3, 0)--(3, 2), c2 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((0, 2)--(3, 2), c3 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((0, 0)--(0, 2), c4 + linewidth(2.2pt));
label("$L_1$", (1.5, 0), S, c1);
label("$L_2$", (3, 1), E, c2);
label("$L_3$", (1.5, 2), N, c3);
label("$L_4$", (0, 1), W, c4);
label("$3$", (3, 0), S);
label("$2$", (0, 2), W);

label("$R$", (2.2, 1.3));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-absolute-extrema-surface-video">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = x^2 - 2xy + 2y</m> over the rectangle <m>R</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-absolute-extrema-surface"
       source="videos/absolute_extrema_surface_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/absolute_extrema_surface_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-equilibrium-stability">
<title>A Physical Application: Equilibrium and Stability</title>

<p>
Consider a marble that rolls on the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> under the
influence of gravity. If we measure heights from the <m>xy</m>-plane,
the potential energy of the marble at the point <m>(x,y)</m> of the
surface is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-potential-energy" number="yes">
U(x,y) = mgz = mg\,f(x,y),
</md>
where <m>m</m> is the mass of the marble and <m>g</m> is the
acceleration due to gravity. The marble is in <term>equilibrium</term>
at a point where the force along the surface vanishes, which happens
exactly where both partial derivatives of the potential energy are
zero:
<md xml:id="eq-skel-equilibrium-condition" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
U_x \amp = mg\,f_x = 0,\\
U_y \amp = mg\,f_y = 0.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>

<p>
Say what <xref ref="eq-skel-equilibrium-condition"/> means
mathematically, and what the second derivative test then tells you
physically.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,8.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {the equilibrium points are:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for what the equilibrium points are.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-marble-stability">
<title>Equilibrium and Stability of a Marble</title>
<statement>
<p>
A marble rests at the origin on each of the following three surfaces:
<md xml:id="eq-skel-three-surfaces" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
\text{(i)} \amp \; z = x^2 + y^2,\\
\text{(ii)} \amp \; z = -x^2 - y^2,\\
\text{(iii)} \amp \; z = \frac{y^2}{3} - \frac{x^2}{3}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<p>
Show that <m>(0,0)</m> is an equilibrium point in each case, and
determine whether the equilibrium is stable or unstable.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Compare with <xref ref="fig-skel-stability-video"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,13.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {(i) \qquad\qquad\qquad (ii) \qquad\qquad\qquad (iii)};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the verdict on each of the three surfaces.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-stability-video">
<caption>
A marble at the origin on each of the three surfaces.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-marble-stability"
       source="videos/stability_marble_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/stability_marble_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<p>
State the general principle these three cases illustrate.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,6.75) {workspace};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {an equilibrium is stable exactly when:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace, with a last row for the general principle.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-stability-cross-sections">
<caption>
Two cross-sections of the potential energy <m>U</m> through an
equilibrium point, with the force on a displaced marble.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>Two parabolas through the origin with force arrows: toward the origin on one, away from it on the other.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Two cross-sections of the potential energy through an equilibrium point at the origin, where a green marble sits. The blue curve opens upward, with force arrows pointing back toward the equilibrium. The red curve opens downward, with force arrows pointing away from it.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-skel-ev-stability-cross-sections"><![CDATA[
// Two cross-sections of the potential energy U through an equilibrium point: one with
// a minimum (the force pushes the marble back) and one with a maximum (it pushes it
// away). The full notes also label them stable and unstable, which is left out here.
import graph;
unitsize(2.3cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

axes(-1.7, 1.7, -1.85, 1.85, "", "$U$");

real stable(real x) { return 0.9*x^2; }
real unstable(real x) { return -0.9*x^2; }
draw(graph(stable, -1.35, 1.35), blue + linewidth(1.6pt));
draw(graph(unstable, -1.35, 1.35), red + linewidth(1.6pt));

// the force on a displaced marble: back towards the equilibrium, or away from it
draw((0.95, 0.35)--(0.5, 0.35), blue + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((-0.95, 0.35)--(-0.5, 0.35), blue + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((0.45, -0.35)--(0.9, -0.35), red + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((-0.45, -0.35)--(-0.9, -0.35), red + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));

dot((0, 0), rgb(0, 0.5, 0) + linewidth(6pt));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<xi:include href="subsec-skel-fmv-why-second-derivative-test.ptx" />

</section>
